<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Halland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Halland is a province on the west coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, formerly held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. It passed to Sweden through seventeenth-century treaties, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> of 1658 concluded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a>. Its acquisition helped establish the boundaries of modern Sweden during the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/halland.jpg" alt="Halland" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Wÿchman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geografisk_karta_%C3%B6ver_landsv%C3%A4garna_genom_Halland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Halland is one of the traditional provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, on the western coast of Götaland, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a>, Småland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>. Its name means Land of Rocky Slabs, from the Swedish hällar, referring to the coastal cliffs of the northern part. The rivers Viskan, Ätran, Nissan and Lagan flow through it to the Kattegat, and it is well known for its fertile soil and as an agricultural district. The Swedish provinces serve no administrative function; that is done by the counties, and Halland County is almost coextensive with the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> was probably a period of relative prosperity in Halland, with over 1,100 tumuli and grave mounds found. Around 200 CE villages began to form larger communities and small kingdoms, and during the 5th and 6th centuries large free-standing farms were created, as at Slöinge. The account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohthere-of-halogaland">Ohthere of Hålogaland</a>, who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> in the 870s, shows Halland to have been Danish then, and it stayed so for most of recorded history. As part of the Scanian lands it came under the Scanian Law and took part in the Scanian Thing, one of the three Things that elected the Danish king.</p><p>From the 13th century Halland was the scene of considerable military action as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and to some degree <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> fought for supremacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and the wars made the province poor. It was invaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a> in 1256, by Magnus III of Sweden in 1277 and by Eric VI of Denmark in 1294. It was split in two along the river Ätran for the next century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war">Northern Seven Years' War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-war">Kalmar War</a> all affected it, and one of the major battles of the Northern Seven Years' War was fought at Axtorna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Second Treaty of Brömsebro</a> in 1645 Halland was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> for a period of thirty years, and the transfer was made permanent by the cession in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658. The last battle in Halland was fought at Fyllebro on 17 August 1676, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a>. Under Danish rule the province had no coat of arms; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> died in 1660, one with a lion had to be created for the newly acquired province, since each province was to be represented by its arms at the royal funeral. Calmer conditions after the wars let the province develop, and farming became one of the more efficient in Sweden by the end of the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Halland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Worse followed in 1658, when Charles X Gustav of Sweden marched his army across the frozen Belts and imposed the Treaty of Roskilde, which took Scania, <b>Halland</b>, Blekinge, Bohuslän, Trøndelag and Bornholm; Copenhagen survived a Swedish assault in 1659, and Trøndelag and Bornholm were returned in 1660.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Roskilde in 1658 surrendered Scania, <b>Halland</b> and Blekinge, the rich eastern provinces, to Sweden, and the Scanian War of 1675–1679 failed to win them back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…(1658), imposed by Charles X Gustav after his celebrated March across the Belts over the frozen straits, Denmark–Norway ceded Scania, Blekinge, <b>Halland</b>, Bornholm, Bohuslän and Trøndelag, the region of Trondheim; Bornholm and Trøndelag were returned in 1660, but the remaining provinces fixed the southern borders of modern Sweden.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
